  "driver": {
    "primary_part": "DRV8411PWPR",
    "primary_lcsc_part": "C18212622",
    "second_source_part": "DRV8833PWPR",
    "second_source_lcsc_part": "C50506",
    "population": "DRV8411_ONLY",
    "population_rationale": "DRV8411 specifies xISEN V_TRIP as 180/200/230 mV and is characterized from VM=1.65 V. The DRV8833 electrical-characteristics table specifies no xISEN trip limits and its datasheet states RDS(on) increases and output current is reduced below VM=5 V, so on a 3.2 V motor rail it is both slower and unspecified in the current-ceiling role. Six 0603 DNP pads keep it available as a drop-in shortage substitute only.",
    "primary_unit_price_usd": 1.18,
    "primary_stock_units": 726,
    "second_source_unit_price_usd": 2.37,
    "second_source_stock_units": 256,
    "driver_cost_per_board_usd": 3.54,
    "second_source_cost_penalty_per_board_usd": 3.57,
    "stock_headroom_boards": 242,
    "selection_constraint": "VM must be specified at the ~3.18 V seen at the driver pin (3.3 V rail less the 0.116 V shunt drop). That alone excludes most of the H-bridge market, which starts at 5 V or 8 V. Adding shunt-programmable current regulation with a specified trip voltage, an open-drain nFAULT and internal input pulldowns narrows the field to one family.",
    "alternatives_evaluated": {
      "DRV8410": "Functional drop-in: same PWP pinout including NC pins 11/14, same VM 1.65-11 V, same xISEN V_TRIP 180/200/230 mV, same nFAULT, nSLEEP 500k and 100k input pulldowns, same t_OFF/t_BLANK/t_DEG. Only RDS(on) differs (800 vs 400 mOhm total), which costs 40 mV instead of 20 mV at 50 mA - immaterial on this rail. Not stocked at LCSC and priced above DRV8411 elsewhere, so it is not a cost-down. It is the best functional second source if it ever appears at LCSC.",
      "DRV8411A": "Same physical pin positions but pins 3/6 are IPROPI current-mirror outputs, not xISEN shunt returns, and TI lists no direct pin-to-pin replacement for it. A 1 ohm resistor there would set a mirror scale, not a trip point, so the shunt-programmable ceiling disappears. Not a drop-in for this topology.",
      "DRV8833_DRV8833C": "The only LCSC-stocked fallback, which is why the six DNP pads are retained. Worse on all four axes: 2.0x the unit price, 0.35x the stock, six extra capacitors, and no specified xISEN trip limits with RDS(on) explicitly derated below VM=5 V.",
      "DRV8841_DRV8842_DRV8844": "Cheaper at LCSC (0.54-0.62 USD) but VM floors of 8.0-8.2 V. Cannot run on a 3.3 V motor rail at all.",
      "DRV8835_DRV8836": "VM from 0 V and 220 mOhm on-resistance, but no current regulation and no fault output. Trades two nested protection layers for well under 1 USD per board.",
      "TC78H660FNG": "Toshiba dual bridge, VM 2.5-16 V, 0.48 ohm, TSSOP16, with an error-flag output. Uses sense-resistor-less internal current detection, so the ceiling is a fixed ~2 A overcurrent protection with no programmable trip and no specified threshold - it removes the 1.5 ohm retune path and the specified-ceiling argument. VM floor is also 2.5 V against DRV8411's 1.65 V."
    },
    "selection_conclusion": "DRV8411 retained. It is simultaneously the cheapest, best-stocked and most capable part that meets the constraint set, so the original cost-down framing was inverted. Driver cost is 3.54 USD of a roughly 43 USD per-board budget, so chasing a cheaper bridge is not where prototype cost lives.",
    "drv8411_populated_placements": 111,
    "drv8833_populated_placements": 117,
    "drv8833_required_capacitors": {
      "vint": "3x 2.2uF 6.3V X5R to GND",
      "vcp": "3x 10nF 50V X7R to VM"
    },
